^  [LINK: (a) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-EB_8-0]   [LINK: (b) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-EB_8-1]  "Encyclopaedia". Encyclopædia Britannica. Archived from the original on December 16, 2010. Retrieved July 27, 2010. An English lexicographer, H.W. Fowler, wrote in the preface to the first edition (1911) of The Concise Oxford Dictionary of Current English language that a dictionary is concerned with the uses of words and phrases and with giving information about the things for which they stand only so far as current use of the words depends upon knowledge of those things. The emphasis in an encyclopedia is much more on the nature of the things for which the words and phrases stand.

^  [LINK: (a) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-DOLei_9-0]   [LINK: (b) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-DOLei_9-1]   [LINK: (c) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-DOLei_9-2]  Hartmann, R. R. K.; James, Gregory (1998). Dictionary of Lexicography. Routledge. p. 49.  [LINK: (ISBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)]   [LINK: (978-0-415-14143-7) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-415-14143-7] . Archived from the original on January 14, 2021. Retrieved July 27, 2010. In contrast with linguistic information, encyclopedia material is more concerned with the description of objective realities than the words or phrases that refer to them. In practice, however, there is no hard and fast boundary between factual and lexical knowledge.

^  [LINK: (a) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-OHEL22_10-0]   [LINK: (b) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encyclopedia#cite_ref-OHEL22_10-1]  Cowie, Anthony Paul (2009). The Oxford History of English Lexicography, Volume I. Oxford University Press. p. 22.  [LINK: (ISBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)]   [LINK: (978-0-415-14143-7) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-415-14143-7] . Archived from the original on April 15, 2021. Retrieved August 17, 2010. An 'encyclopedia' (encyclopaedia) usually gives more information than a dictionary; it explains not only the words but also the things and concepts referred to by the words.

